facts:
**Non-convex**: at least one interior angle is reflex, so a vertex points back into the shape.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\usetikzlibrary{angles, quotes}
\coordinate (A) at (0,0);
\coordinate (B) at (2.2,-1.25);
\coordinate (C) at (4.4,0);
\coordinate (D) at (2.2,3);
\draw (A) -- (B) -- (C) -- (D) -- cycle;
\pic[draw, "$<180^{\circ}$"{font=\small}, angle radius=0.5cm, angle eccentricity=1.6] {angle = C--B--A};
\node at (2.2,-2) {Convex};
\coordinate (P) at (6.2,0);
\coordinate (Q) at (8.4,1.05);
\coordinate (R) at (10.6,0);
\coordinate (S) at (8.4,3);
\draw (P) -- (Q) -- (R) -- (S) -- cycle;
\pic[draw, "$>180^{\circ}$"{font=\small}, angle radius=0.5cm, angle eccentricity=1.6] {angle = R--Q--P};
\node at (8.4,-2) {Non-convex};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

In a non-convex quadrilateral one diagonal lies outside the shape, so the two diagonals do not cross inside it.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\coordinate (A) at (0,0);
\coordinate (B) at (2.2,-1.25);
\coordinate (C) at (4.4,0);
\coordinate (D) at (2.2,3);
\draw (A) -- (B) -- (C) -- (D) -- cycle;
\draw[dashed] (A) -- (C);
\draw[dashed] (B) -- (D);
\node at (2.2,-2) {Convex};
\coordinate (P) at (6.2,0);
\coordinate (Q) at (8.4,1.05);
\coordinate (R) at (10.6,0);
\coordinate (S) at (8.4,3);
\draw (P) -- (Q) -- (R) -- (S) -- cycle;
\draw[dashed] (P) -- (R);
\draw[dashed] (Q) -- (S);
\node at (8.4,-2) {Non-convex};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

